AB  - SubjectsNine randomized controlled trials (RCTs) were included out of 25 RCTs reviewed. Trials were excluded primarily over concern about bias. The 9 trials included collectively represented 501 patients, all of whom had a diagnosis of oral leukoplakia. The definition used for leukoplakia was derived from consensus conferences.[1], [2] and [3]ExposureThere were various active interventions (exposures) reviewed: surgical removal, topical medical treatment, systemic medical treatment, removal of predisposing habits, photodynamic treatment, and combination treatments. These were compared with control groups that received either placebo or no treatment.Topical treatments included bleomycin and retinoic acid and ketorolac oral rinse. Systemic treatments included beta carotene, vitamin A (retinoids), tea, and lycopene.Main Outcome MeasureThe primary outcomes of interest were malignant transformation of leukoplakia (morbidity), demonstrated by histopathological examination. Secondary outcomes included variation in histological features among already dysplastic lesions, long-term resolution of lesions, and relapse (in studies aimed at lesion elimination). When reported, safety was evaluated by measures of the incidence of adverse effects. Acceptability was measured by the rate at which patients dropped out of the study.Main ResultsTwo studies (172 patients) provided data on the rate of malignant transformation. These studies evaluated bleomycin, systemic vitamin A, and systemic beta carotene. None of the treatments showed a benefit when compared with placebo.All 9 studies reported on clinical resolution of the lesions. No benefit was found for bleomycin, ketorolac, and tea. Two studies showed a benefit from systemic treatment with beta carotene and lycopene (Risk Ratio = 0.69; 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.54 to 0.88). Five studies evaluated vitamin A or other retinoids and found a small effect (Risk Ratio = 0.69; 95% CI 0.50 to 0.96).Three studies reported histological changes with comparison groups. Both retinoic acid (Risk Ratio = 0.51; 95% CI 0.32 to 0.81) and lycopene (Risk Ratio = 0.25; 95% CI 0.12 to 0.46) showed significant improvement.Adverse effects were reported commonly for topical bleomycin, systemic retinoic acid, ketorolac oral rinse, vitamin A, and beta carotene. The effects varied but were severe enough in some cases to cause patients to withdraw from the study. These included severe conjunctivitis and hypertrigliceridemia (retinoic acid) and intolerable mouth pain (ketorolac oral rinse). However, overall acceptability of most treatments was considered good, as drop-out rates were similar between treatment and comparison groups.ConclusionThe authors conclude that none of the treatments were effective in preventing malignant transformation of leukoplakias. In the cases where healing was evident, relapse was common. None of the treatments evaluated should be considered as definitive treatment of oral leukoplakia and, consequently, all patients, regardless of the intervention provided, should receive regular follow-up evaluations.
